{% extends 'help/article.html.twig' %}

{% block body %}
    <p>
        Every load with geocoded stops gets a route: how far it is, how long it takes, and when
        the truck should arrive. The number that matters most is the one most systems get wrong,
        so it is worth understanding where it comes from.
    </p>

    <h2>Loaded and empty miles</h2>
    <p>
        The app counts two kinds of distance separately, because they are not the same money:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Loaded miles</strong> — the route through the stops, with freight on.</li>
        <li><strong>Empty miles</strong> (deadhead) — from where the truck is now to the first pickup.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Total miles is the two added together. Keeping them apart is what lets you see that a
        well-paying load stopped paying once the truck drove two hundred empty miles to get it.
    </p>

    <h2>Routed versus estimated</h2>
    <p>
        Mileage comes from one of two places, and the app always labels which:
    </p>
    <div class="help-scroll">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Source</th><th>How it is measured</th><th>Accuracy</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Routed</strong></td>
                    <td>Real road distance, via Google Routes</td>
                    <td>Billable</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Estimated</strong></td>
                    <td>Straight line between coordinates</td>
                    <td>Runs roughly 15–25% under true road miles</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="warn">
        <p><strong>Do not bill from estimated miles.</strong> A straight line ignores every mountain,
        river and interstate interchange between the two points. If you see "est." on a load, connect
        Google Maps with the Routes capability enabled — see
        <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'integrations' }) }}">Integrations</a> — and the figure
        is recalculated as a real route.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>ETAs</h2>
    <p>
        Alongside distance you get drive time and an arrival estimate for the pickup and the
        delivery. These are driving time from the route, not an hours-of-service projection — they
        do not know about the driver's remaining hours, breaks or a ten-hour reset. Use them to
        sanity-check an appointment window, not to promise one.
    </p>

    <h2>Addresses</h2>
    <p>
        Stop addresses autocomplete as you type and are converted to coordinates when saved. That
        geocoding is what everything else hangs off — without coordinates there is no route, no
        deadhead, no proximity in <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'assigning-drivers' }) }}">driver
        suggestions</a> and no weather. If a stop shows no route, check that its address resolved
        rather than being typed as free text.
    </p>

    <h2>Weather at the stop</h2>
    <p>
        Each stop with coordinates carries a four-day outlook: high and low, and a reading for the
        appointment window itself — average temperature, the worst conditions expected and the
        chance of rain during the window. It needs no account or API key.
    </p>
    <p>
        It is most useful on temperature-sensitive freight and on winter mountain crossings, where
        knowing the window is the difference between routing around weather and sitting in it.
    </p>
{% endblock %}
